  "success_stories": "Following the Monterey Park mass shooting, ALPR technology provided investigators with critical investigative leads by helping identify and track the suspect vehicle. The information assisted law enforcement in locating the vehicle in a neighboring jurisdiction and supported the rapid coordination of the regional homicide investigation, demonstrating the value of ALPR technology in solving major violent crimes.\n\nAn off duty Monterey Park Police officer was fatally shot during an attempted carjacking by three suspects. Within hours of the homicide, investigators used Automated License Plate Reader (ALPR) data to locate the suspect vehicle in a neighboring jurisdiction. The ALPR information provided critical investigative leads that helped detectives identify, locate, and arrest the suspects. This case demonstrates how ALPR technology can rapidly generate investigative leads and assist law enforcement in solving violent crimes while working in conjunction with traditional investigative methods.\n\nA disgruntled ex-boyfriend began to stalk and harass his ex-girlfriend. The suspect\u2019s erratic behavior escalated, which eventually led him to burglarize the victim\u2019s home. The victim and her children no longer felt safe and temporarily left their home. Using ALPR, detectives were able to build a case to place the suspect in the crime scene during the commission of the crime. The invaluable investigative lead that the ALPR provided eventually led to the apprehension of the suspect.\n\nA 90-year-old woman was walking home when 3 suspects approached her and began stealing items from her person. The suspects used force to take items from the victim, and the victim was in fear for her life. Video footage depicted the suspect vehicle having a distinct white sticker on the rear window of the vehicle. Using ALPR, detectives located the vehicle with the identical sticker. Using this lead, detectives were later able to identify the suspect vehicle license plate and eventually the suspects themselves. The suspects were later arrested for robbery.\n\nAn elderly male with dementia was reported missing by his wife. The elderly male left his residence in his vehicle during early morning hours to get fast food and never returned home. The missing person's license plate was entered into ALPR, and the missing person\u2019s vehicle was detected by ALPR cameras in Riverside County. With the use of ALPR, we were able to continue to monitor the missing person\u2019s vehicle whereabouts. Officers eventually located the elderly missing person in the Long Beach area. The elderly male said he had gotten lost and forgot how to get back home. The use of ALPR cameras allowed law enforcement to locate the missing person and reunite him with his family.\n\nOver the span of one month, at least 7 different vehicles were broken into, by the same suspects, at 3 different locations in Monterey Park. Using ALPR, detectives discovered the suspect vehicle\u2019s pattern of travel, as well as the dates and times of the crimes. ALPR helped establish a path of travel on Thursdays, from Northern California to Monterey Park. Utilizing ALPR, it was discovered that the suspect vehicle was once again traveling southbound from Northern California, and entering Monterey Park at nighttime hours. Suspects were once again captured on surveillance cameras breaking into vehicles. ALPR assisted officers in locating the suspect vehicle that night, and 3 suspects were taken into custody and a total of 16 Felony counts were filed between the 3 suspects.\n\nMPPD officers received multiple calls for service regarding a male shooting at passing vehicles. Further investigation revealed that the suspect and his girlfriend were engaged in a verbal argument. As the suspect and his girlfriend argued, the suspect retrieved a firearm and shot at a passing vehicle, striking an uninvolved victim\u2019s driver's side door. The suspect then entered the vehicle with his girlfriend and fled the area. With the assistance of ALPR, the vehicle was identified, which provided an investigative lead to identify the suspect who committed the shooting. The suspect was later arrested, convicted, and sentenced to 18 years in prison.\n\nA male suspect opened the door of an unlocked vehicle and stole items including money, IDs, and bank cards. The suspect is later captured on camera using the victim\u2019s cards at a nearby gas station. Video surveillance captured a white pickup truck parked next to the victim's vehicle at the time of the incident. Using ALPR, detectives identified the suspect vehicle's license plate, leading to the suspect's arrest. Multiple Felony counts were filed against the suspect, and stolen items were returned to the victim.\n\nA suspect shattered the window of a locked vehicle and stole over 1,000 dollars in tools. The suspect\u2019s pickup truck and a description of the suspect were captured on camera. The suspect's pickup truck had a bed cover and unique airbrushed graphics on the sides. Using ALPR, detectives identified the suspect vehicle with an identical airbrushed paint job. Based on this, detectives were able to generate a lead, which later revealed the suspect's identity. The vehicle was later located with the male suspect driving it, leading to his arrest.\n\nA juvenile was walking to school and was threatened by a convicted felon. The juvenile was able to identify the vehicle used and provided additional identifying information. By reviewing ALPR data, detectives were able to identify the suspect\u2019s vehicle and track its movements across the city. The camera hits allowed investigators to establish a timeline, pinpoint the suspect\u2019s likely travel patterns, and determine his current location. Armed with this information, officers coordinated a safe approach and ultimately apprehended the individual without incident. Thanks to the timely use of automated license plate cameras, investigators were able to quickly locate the suspect, prevent further harm, and bring a dangerous individual into custody.\n\nAn adult male was accused of committing lewd acts with a child, then threatened to take his own life and fled the scene with firearms. Immediately after the suspect fled, officers entered the vehicle\u2019s license plate information into the ALPR system. Within minutes, the cameras detected the suspect\u2019s vehicle traveling across multiple jurisdictions. By monitoring the vehicle\u2019s movement in real time, detectives were able to track the suspect\u2019s direction of travel and identify a predictable pattern. Working collaboratively with patrol units in the corresponding areas, detectives coordinated a safe and strategic containment. The suspect was taken into custody without incident. This case highlights how ALPR technology significantly enhances investigative capabilities, accelerates suspect location efforts, and improves public safety outcomes.\n\nOfficers responded to a report of shots fired at an occupied residence. Surveillance footage captured a vehicle driving past the location at the time the shots were fired; however, the license plate was not visible. During the follow-up investigation, detectives used ALPR data to identify vehicles matching the description in the area at the time of the shooting. Through additional analysis, detectives located the vehicle, identified the responsible party, and later arrested the suspect.\n\nPatrol officers contacted a suspicious vehicle and discovered burglary tools, stolen personal identifying information, and evidence of identity theft. The suspect was arrested for those offenses. During the follow-up investigation, detectives used ALPR data to link the vehicle to a separate recent vehicle burglary in Monterey Park. Additional property stolen during that burglary was later located inside the suspect vehicle.\n\nAn elderly male was targeted in the grocery store by 2 male suspects and 1 male suspect getaway driver. The suspects approached the victim and forcefully removed his gold chain worth approximately $5,000. The suspects entered a distinct vehicle, and using ALPR data, a license plate for the suspect vehicle was obtained. After further investigation, it was revealed that these suspects were involved in approximately 10 other robberies in Los Angeles County, using multiple vehicles. The use of ALPR assisted in identifying all the suspect vehicles involved in this string of robberies, which later resulted in 4 arrests and multiple felony counts filed.\n\nThe suspect entered Home Depot and stole merchandise worth over $950. Detectives used ALPR data to determine that the suspect vehicle was at the Marketplace and observed the stolen property inside. Further investigation revealed the suspect was involved in more than 15 Home Depot theft cases. The suspect was identified and arrested, and the case was filed with the DA\u2019s Office's Organized Crime Division for commercial burglary.\n\nA grand theft occurred at the Guitar Center\u2014the theft of a guitar worth more than $950. The employee photographed the suspect vehicle's license plate and stated the same suspect had shoplifted in 2024. Using ALPR data, detectives were able to confirm that the suspect vehicle entered/exited the Marketplace and his place of residence based on his route of travel. With the help of the suspect\u2019s parents, the suspect turned himself in to MPPD and confessed to all crimes. Multiple counts of felony theft were filed.\n\nThe suspect entered Costco and stole merchandise worth under $950. An employee photographed the suspect vehicle's license plate, and detectives were able to confirm the suspect vehicle entered/exited the Marketplace. Further investigation revealed the suspect was on probation for theft in Orange County and was ultimately arrested at her probation appointment. The DA filed the case, and the suspect was sentenced to 16 months in state prison based on her numerous theft priors.\nData & Usage Summary",
